## Token/Code Expiry (Database-Enforced)

**What it is.** Every verification code and password reset token carries an `expiresAt` timestamp, checked against the current time on the server at the moment it's used — not just displayed as a countdown in the interface.

**Why it is needed.** A countdown shown in the browser is just decoration; nothing stops a user (or an attacker with a captured code) from ignoring it and submitting the code late. If expiry isn't enforced where the code is actually checked, it isn't enforced at all.

**How I implemented it.** Both `EmailVerificationToken` and `PasswordResetToken` store `expiresAt` in the database. The verify and reset routes check `expiresAt` against `Date.now()` before accepting either one — the same lookup that already filters for unused/unexpired records.

```ts
// src/app/api/auth/verify/route.ts (excerpt)
const token = await prisma.emailVerificationToken.findFirst({
  where: { userId: user.id, usedAt: null },
  orderBy: { createdAt: "desc" },
});

if (token.expiresAt.getTime() < Date.now()) {
  return NextResponse.json(
    { error: "That code has expired. Request a new one." },
    { status: 400 }
  );
}
```

```ts
// src/app/api/auth/password-reset/route.ts (excerpt)
if (
  !record ||
  record.usedAt !== null ||
  record.expiresAt.getTime() < Date.now()
) {
  return NextResponse.json(
    { error: "This reset link is invalid or has expired." },
    { status: 400 }
  );
}
```

**What I chose against, and why.** I chose against relying on the client-side countdown as anything more than a display — I considered skipping server-side expiry checks to save a query, but that would mean the interface's timer is the only thing enforcing expiry, which is not enforcement, it's theatre.

