## Idempotency

**What it is.** An idempotent endpoint produces the same end result no matter how many times the same request is submitted — one signup request sent twice should still result in exactly one account.

**Why it is needed.** Without it, a double-click, a slow network retry, or a user hitting "back" and resubmitting a form creates a second account, a second charge, or a second copy of whatever the request was creating — silently, with no error to signal anything went wrong.

**How I implemented it.** Signup relies on the unique constraint on `User.email` rather than a pre-check-then-insert pattern (which would have a race condition between the check and the insert). When the insert throws Prisma's `P2002` unique-constraint error, the code catches that specific error and falls back to the existing account — either answering `alreadyVerified: true` for a confirmed account, or stopping to re-issue a fresh verification code for it, which is what the user sees on a repeat signup.

```ts
// src/app/api/auth/signup/route.ts (excerpt)
let user;
try {
  user = await prisma.user.create({ data: { email, passwordHash, fullName } });
} catch (error) {
  // Unique constraint on email: the same signup submitted twice must not
  // create a second account or throw a 500.
  if (
    error instanceof Prisma.PrismaClientKnownRequestError &&
    error.code === "P2002"
  ) {
    const existing = await prisma.user.findUnique({ where: { email } });
    if (!existing) throw error;
    if (existing.emailVerifiedAt) {
      return NextResponse.json({ alreadyVerified: true }, { status: 200 });
    }
    user = existing;
  } else {
    throw error;
  }
}
```

Both the fresh and the reused account then fall through to the same shared block that stores a new verification code and updates `emailVerificationLastSentAt`:

```ts
// src/app/api/auth/signup/route.ts (excerpt)
const code = generateOtp();
const now = new Date();
await prisma.$transaction([
  prisma.emailVerificationToken.create({
    data: {
      userId: user.id,
      code: code,
      codeHash: hashToken(code),
      expiresAt: new Date(now.getTime() + VERIFICATION_CODE_TTL_MS),
    },
  }),
  prisma.user.update({
    where: { id: user.id },
    data: { emailVerificationLastSentAt: now },
  }),
]);

await sendVerificationEmail(email, code);
```

**What I chose against, and why.** I chose against checking "does this email already exist" before inserting and trusting that check alone — two requests arriving close together could both pass the check before either has inserted, creating a duplicate anyway. Relying on the database's own constraint as the actual enforcement point closes that race condition; the `P2002` catch is just how I respond to it gracefully rather than crashing.

---

## Database Constraints as Last Line of Defence

**What it is.** A constraint declared on the database itself — a unique constraint, a foreign key, a non-nullable column — enforced by the database engine regardless of what the application code does or fails to do.

**Why it is needed.** Application code has bugs, and any check written in application code can be skipped by a code path nobody thought to test — a new endpoint added later, a race condition, a migration that runs application code out of order. A constraint at the database level holds even when the code above it doesn't.

**How I implemented it.** `email` on `User` is `@unique`; `tokenHash` on `PasswordResetToken` is `@unique`; every foreign key uses `onDelete: Cascade`; `passwordHash` is required (non-nullable). Each of these is the actual backstop for a rule the application code also tries to enforce.

```prisma
// prisma/schema.prisma (excerpt)
model User {
  id           String   @id @default(cuid())
  email        String   @unique          // one account per email
  passwordHash String                     // required, never nullable
  fullName     String
  emailVerifiedAt DateTime?
  emailVerificationLastSentAt DateTime?
  createdAt    DateTime @default(now())
  updatedAt    DateTime @updatedAt
  sessions               Session[]
  emailVerificationTokens EmailVerificationToken[]
  passwordResetTokens    PasswordResetToken[]
}

model EmailVerificationToken {
  id        String    @id @default(cuid())
  userId    String
  user      User      @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  code      String
  codeHash  String
  expiresAt DateTime
  usedAt    DateTime?
  createdAt DateTime  @default(now())
  @@index([userId])
}

model PasswordResetToken {
  id        String    @id @default(cuid())
  userId    String
  user      User      @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  tokenHash String    @unique
  expiresAt DateTime
  usedAt    DateTime?
  createdAt DateTime  @default(now())
  @@index([userId])
}

model Session {
  id        String   @id @default(cuid())
  userId    String
  user      User     @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  expiresAt DateTime
  createdAt DateTime @default(now())
  @@index([userId])
}
```

**What I chose against, and why.** I chose against relying solely on application-level checks (e.g. "look up by email before inserting") as the only safeguard. As shown in the idempotency case above, that approach alone has a real gap under concurrent requests; the database constraint is what actually closes it.

---

## Protected Routes

**What it is.** A route that requires a valid, checked session before it renders anything or returns any data — as opposed to a route that merely hides a link or a button in the interface for logged-out users.

**Why it is needed.** Hiding a dashboard link in the UI does nothing to stop someone from typing the dashboard's URL directly, or hitting its underlying API route with curl. Without a server-side check, the "protection" is purely cosmetic.

**How I implemented it.** `src/app/dashboard/page.tsx` performs the same session lookup used everywhere else, on every render — since it's a server component marked `force-dynamic`, this re-runs on every request, including a direct URL visit with no prior navigation. No valid session redirects to `/signin` before any dashboard content is returned.

```ts
// src/app/dashboard/page.tsx (excerpt)
export const dynamic = "force-dynamic";

export default async function DashboardPage() {
  if (!isAuthConfigured()) {
    redirect("/signin");
  }

  const user = await getSessionUser();

  // Server-side session check: no valid session (even a direct URL hit with
  // no referrer) bounces straight back to /signin.
  if (!user) {
    redirect("/signin");
  }

  // ...dashboard markup
}
```

The underlying guard reads the session cookie, verifies its signature, and checks the row + expiry in the database (see `getSessionUser` under "Session Management"); a missing, tampered, or expired session all resolve to `null` and then to the redirect.

**What I chose against, and why.** I chose against a client-side-only redirect (checking auth state in the browser after the page has already loaded) — that approach still sends the full page's data to the browser before deciding to hide it, and can be bypassed by disabling JavaScript or intercepting the request directly.


## Client-side Form State Persistence

**What it is.** A central client-side store that owns the non-sensitive values shared across every auth form (emails, full name, which modal is open, the pending-verification email) and mirrors changes to `sessionStorage` so they survive reloads and navigation within the tab.

**Why it is needed.** The auth flows connect to each other — you sign up, get bounced to verify, and the verification screen already knows your email; you type a wrong email on sign-in, hit "Forgot password?", and the reset form carries that email over. Without a shared, persisted store, each form would only know what was typed into it, and every reload would lose the user's place mid-flow.

**How I implemented it.** `src/lib/auth-store.tsx` is a React Context + `useReducer` store (no external state library). Each state-changing action writes through a persistence wrapper that also mirrors the value to `sessionStorage`. `src/lib/form-storage.ts` is a tiny, defensive wrapper around `sessionStorage` (server-safe, `try/catch` for private browsing) so the app degrades gracefully to plain form behavior when storage is unavailable.

```ts
// src/lib/form-storage.ts (excerpt)
const PREFIX = "scope_form.";

export const KEYS = {
  signinEmail: `${PREFIX}signin.email`,
  signupFullName: `${PREFIX}signup.fullName`,
  signupEmail: `${PREFIX}signup.email`,
  forgotEmail: `${PREFIX}forgot.email`,
} as const;

export function saveFormValue(key: string, value: string): void {
  if (typeof window === "undefined") return;
  try {
    sessionStorage.setItem(key, value);
  } catch {
    // Storage unavailable — nothing to persist, keep the form usable.
  }
}
```

```ts
// src/lib/auth-store.tsx (excerpt) — persistence mirrors every dispatch
const persistAndDispatch = useCallback(
  (action: AuthAction) => {
    switch (action.type) {
      case "SET_PENDING_EMAIL":
        saveFormValue(PENDING_EMAIL_KEY, action.email);
        break;
      case "SET_SIGNIN_EMAIL":
        if (action.email) saveFormValue(KEYS.signinEmail, action.email);
        else clearFormValue(KEYS.signinEmail);
        break;
      // ...SET_SIGNUP_FULL_NAME, SET_SIGNUP_EMAIL, SET_FORGOT_EMAIL,
      //     CLEAR_SIGNUP, CLEAR_PENDING_EMAIL all mirror the same way
    }
    dispatch(action);
  },
  [dispatch]
);
```

The cross-flow wiring that makes the persistence useful:

```ts
// src/components/auth/forgot-password-modal.tsx (excerpt)
// Carry over the email typed on the sign-in form into the reset flow.
useEffect(() => {
  if (!state.forgotEmail && state.signinEmail) {
    actions.setForgotEmail(state.signinEmail);
  }
}, [state.forgotEmail, state.signinEmail, actions]);
```

The stored values are cleared when the owning flow completes — signout of the sign-in email on success, `clearPendingEmail()` + `clearSignup()` after verification succeeds, `clearSignup()` after account creation.

**What I chose against, and why.** I chose against persisting passwords, password confirmations, or OTP codes — those are held only in local component state and never written to `sessionStorage`, so a session-recovery or XSS read of storage can't harvest credentials. I also chose `sessionStorage` over `localStorage` deliberately: the values are per-tab and die with the tab, which is a smaller persistence window than the browser-global `localStorage` and matches "this flow is in progress right now."

