6. What Went Wrong
## 6. What Went Wrong

### Problem 1: Signing out landed on the "Verify Email" screen instead of Sign In

**Symptom.** After clicking "Sign out," I landed on the email
verification screen instead of the sign-in page. It looked like sign-out
hadn't actually worked, or like my account was stuck half logged-in.

**Investigation.** I checked whether the session was actually being
cleared — it was, the cookie was gone and the session row was deleted
from the database. So sign-out itself was working fine. The issue turned
out to be about routing, not sessions.

Sign-in and verify-email aren't separate pages in my app — they're the
same shared component, `sign-in-screen.tsx`, which opens different modals
depending on which route loaded it. The `/verify-email` route tells that
component to auto-open the verify modal on load:

```ts
// src/app/(auth)/verify-email/page.tsx
export default function VerifyEmailPage() {
  return <SignInScreen preload="verify" />;
}
```

So landing on `/verify-email` after sign-out will always show the verify
modal, regardless of whether the user is actually mid-verification or
not.

**Cause.** Sign-out was sending the user back to `/verify-email` instead
of `/signin`. It wasn't a bug in the sign-out logic itself, just the
wrong redirect target.

**Fix.** Sign-out now redirects to `/signin`, which has no preload and no
modal attached:

```ts
// src/app/dashboard/sign-out-button.tsx
const handleSignOut = async () => {
  setPending(true);
  await fetch("/api/auth/signout", { method: "POST" }).catch(() => undefined);
  router.push("/signin");
  router.refresh();
};
```

I also made the sign-out route itself safe to call more than once: it
always clears the cookie, and only deletes the session row from the
database if a valid cookie was actually there. Clicking sign-out twice,
or the request failing partway, can't leave anything in a broken state.

---

### Problem 2: A wrong password gave no visible feedback

**Symptom.** Typing the wrong password and submitting looked like
nothing happened. No error, no shake, nothing changed on screen.

**Investigation.** I first assumed the server wasn't sending an error
back. Checking the network tab showed that was wrong — the server was
returning a 401 with a message every time:

```
HTTP 401
{ "error": "Invalid email or password." }
```

(The message is intentionally vague — it doesn't say which part was
wrong, so someone can't use it to check which emails have accounts.)

So the server was fine. The problem was the frontend — the error was
only shown in a toast in the bottom-right corner that disappeared after
about 3.5 seconds. My eyes were on the password field, not the corner of
the screen, so I never even noticed it firing the first few times I
tested it.

**Cause.** The error existed and was correct. It just wasn't shown
anywhere the user would actually look.

**Fix.** The sign-in form now shows the error inline, right above the
submit button:

```tsx
// src/components/auth/sign-in-form.tsx
{formError && (
  <div role="alert" className="...">
    {formError}
  </div>
)}
```

I kept the toast for one case — redirecting an unverified user to the
verify screen — because that involves an actual page change and the
message needs to survive it. For a wrong password there's no navigation,
so an inline message that just stays put made more sense.

Checked this by testing both cases directly: wrong password shows the
inline box, correct password signs in and redirects, no console errors
either way.

---

### Problem 3: The image on the sign-in screen stopped centering correctly

**Symptom.** After reworking the sign-in screen's layout, the branded
image on the side started looking cropped or off-center at some screen
widths.

**Investigation.** I checked whether a CSS class had gotten deleted
somewhere — it hadn't, everything was still there. Looking back at how
the image area used to be built, I found the actual issue: it used to be
drawn with pure CSS, not an actual image, and was given a fixed size:

```css
/* old version */
.brand-art-card {
  width: 380px;
  height: 560px;   /* fixed size */
}
```

At some point that CSS artwork got swapped for a real PNG, but the fixed
width/height from the old version stayed as-is.

**Cause.** The actual image has its own proportions — roughly 2265:2544,
noticeably taller than wide. Forcing that into a box with the wrong
fixed dimensions stretches or crops it, which is exactly what I was
seeing.

**Fix.** The container now sizes itself off the image's real aspect
ratio instead of a fixed guess, and uses `object-contain` so the whole
image always stays visible:

```tsx
// src/components/brand-art-card.tsx
<div className="relative mx-auto aspect-[2265/2544] w-[92%] max-w-[380px] ...">
  <Image
    src="/images/side-screen-v2.png"
    alt="SCOPE - a preview of the product"
    fill
    className="object-contain"
  />
</div>
```

I also made the width responsive — smaller on mobile, wider and shifted
slightly on desktop — so it holds up across screen sizes instead of just
the one I was looking at when I fixed it.

One thing I noticed after: a leftover comment in the code still described
the old behaviour ("object-cover") even though the class had already been
changed to `object-contain`. I'd used an AI coding assistant for this
part, and that's the kind of stale leftover it can leave behind when it
changes an approach mid-edit — worth catching, since a comment that
doesn't match the code can mislead whoever reads it next, including me.


